    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d80-a2">

                <pb facs="0273" n="203" xml:id="pg203"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No. 151.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wingate to Mr. Young</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Foochow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-03-02"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 2,
                        1883</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reply to the inquiry contained in your
                    dispatch of the 23d January, 1883, No. 13, I have the honor to report that a war
                    Vessel, the Kai Chi, was launched at the Foochow arsenal on the 11th January
                    last, and is now in process of completion, although it is not expected to be
                    ready for sea for some six months.</p>
                <p>I am not able to give you the details of its size and armament, but eight Krupp
                    guns intended for it are reported to be now on their way, one from Germany.</p>
                <p>This vessel was designed by, and built under the superintendence of the Chinese
                    who were educated in France and returned to Foochow some three years, or less,
                    ago.</p>
                <p>Aside from some portions of the machinery and the iron plates, the whole work has
                    been done at the arsenal.</p>
                <p>I understand that four similar vessels are to be built, hut that the laying of
                    the keels is delayed owing to the fact that the imperial commissioner left
                    immediately after the launching of the Kai Chi, and a new commissioner has not
                    been appointed.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>J. C. A. WINGATE,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States
                            Consul.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
